<article class="env-lemma" id="05UM">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05UM">Lemma <span data-tag="05UM">94.15.4</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme contained in $\mathit{Sch}_{fppf}$. Let $\mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ be a morphism of stacks in groupoids over $(\mathit{Sch}/S)_{fppf}$. Assume that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$\mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ is representable by algebraic spaces, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\mathcal{Y}$ is an algebraic stack over $S$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $\mathcal{X}$ is an algebraic stack over $S$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\mathcal{V} \to \mathcal{Y}$ be a surjective smooth $1$-morphism from a representable stack in groupoids to $\mathcal{Y}$. This exists by Definition <a href="/tag/026O" data-tag="026O">94.12.1</a>. Then the $2$-fibre product $\mathcal{U} = \mathcal{V} \times _{\mathcal Y} \mathcal X$ is representable by an algebraic space by Lemma <a href="/tag/0300" data-tag="0300">94.9.8</a>. The $1$-morphism $\mathcal{U} \to \mathcal X$ is representable by algebraic spaces, smooth, and surjective, see Lemmas <a href="/tag/0302" data-tag="0302">94.9.7</a> and <a href="/tag/045C" data-tag="045C">94.10.6</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/05UL" data-tag="05UL">94.15.3</a> we conclude that $\mathcal{X}$ is an algebraic stack. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>